('bivaltypquestionnaire', '200 BivalTyp BivalTyp Home Project â\x86\x93 Project description Questionnaire Instructions for\xa0contributors Team Publications How to read the data Languages â\x86\x93 List view Map view Predicates Data overview â\x86\x93 Patterns overview Data in one spreadsheet Maps Download Questionnaire General Each entry in the questionnaire consists of a number (1â\x80\x93130), an English name of the predicate (referred to as â\x80\x9cPredicateâ\x80\x9d, e.g., â\x80\x98be afraidâ\x80\x99, â\x80\x98throwâ\x80\x99, etc.), and a stimulus sentence used in elicitation.\nSome stimulus sentences are accompanied by context information provided in parentheses. These pieces of information are intended as semantic cues that make the described situations more specific. For example, in 101, the sentence â\x80\x9cP. shot at the birdâ\x80\x9d is accompanied by the comment â\x80\x9c(He missed.)â\x80\x9d. This means that the translation equivalent of â\x80\x98shootâ\x80\x99 should be compatible with the idea that the shot went wide, but â\x80\x98he missedâ\x80\x99 should not necessarily be translated into the target language.\nâ\x80\x9cP.â\x80\x9d and â\x80\x9cM.â\x80\x9d stand for person names (or else other human noun phrases) that the contributor might choose in accordance with the cultural context of the investigated language. Each stimulus', [('Download', 'https://bivaltyp.info/download/'), ('here', 'https://bivaltyp.info/docs/questionnaire_russian.pdf'), ('here', 'https://bivaltyp.info/docs/Questionnaire_FR_delta_2023_08.pdf'), ('here', 'https://bivaltyp.info/docs/Questionnaire_DE_alpha_2023_05.pdf')])
